body.p:
The Office Action cites Gurewitsch as teaching a device for measuring bioimpedance of cervical tissue. The Office acknowledges that Gurewitsch does not appear to explicitly teach the apparatus capable of determining a force Ftip. The Office Action alleges that it would have been obvious to replace the spring arrangement of Gurewitsch (as shown in Fig. 12 and described inSVG
{EMBED HERE}
0.150.0770102) with the force sensor and associated components of Anthony (e.g. accelerometer, etc.), for the purpose of obtaining quantitative data about the conditions under which the device was used (Anthony:  005), including data on the degree of axial loading on the probe tip, to thereby account for the conditions based on quantitative analysis and processing (Anthony:   0084), instead of relying on maintaining a "general" continuity of contact using the spring of Gurewitsch (  0102).

